---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/data/site';
import { url } from '@/lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SCADA interface — ind-ds"
  description="Build the UI layer of a SCADA interface with ind-ds: asset hierarchies, alarm summaries, process values, equipment faceplates and trends — over your own data acquisition."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Use case"
    title="SCADA interface"
    lead="A SCADA screen supervises many assets at once and lets an operator move from an overview to the detail of a single pump or line. ind-ds supplies the navigation, faceplates, values and alarm views — as the interface layer, not the data system."
    crumbs={[
      { label: 'Home', href: url('/') },
      { label: 'Use cases', href: url('/use-cases') },
      { label: 'SCADA interface' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container stack uc-detail">
      <h2>Typical environment</h2>
      <p>
        A supervisory workstation, often browser-based, watching a process area or a whole plant.
        Screens are larger than a machine panel and updated continuously as values arrive from the
        acquisition layer. Operators keep the view open for a full shift, so density, legibility and
        stable layout under changing data all matter.
      </p>

      <h2>User roles</h2>
      <p>
        A control operator monitors the process and acknowledges alarms; a shift supervisor reviews
        area status and trends; a process or automation engineer inspects individual assets when
        diagnosing behaviour. Each moves between an overview and equipment-level detail.
      </p>

      <h2>Interface challenges</h2>
      <p>
        Many assets must be legible at once without visual chaos. Navigation has to reflect the plant
        hierarchy so operators can drill from area to unit to equipment. Alarm summaries must
        prioritise the abnormal and stay honest about acknowledgement state. Process values need
        consistent units and formatting, and moving from overview to a detailed faceplate should feel
        continuous rather than like a different application.
      </p>

      <h2>How ind-ds helps</h2>
      <p>
        For structure, <code>ind-sidebar-nav</code>, <code>ind-tree-node</code> and
        <code>ind-breadcrumb-item</code> express the asset hierarchy and the overview-to-detail path.
        <code>ind-asset-overview</code> and <code>ind-equipment-dashboard</code> aggregate many assets,
        while equipment components such as <code>ind-pump-card</code> and
        <code>ind-tank-level-card</code> act as faceplates for a single unit. Alarms are handled by
        <code>ind-alarm-summary</code> and <code>ind-alarm-panel</code>, which are ISA-18.2-aware
        (HH/H/L/LL), support acknowledge and timestamps, and sort unacknowledged items first. Process
        values use <code>ind-value</code>, <code>ind-gauge</code> and <code>ind-kpi-card</code>, and
        inline trends use <code>ind-sparkline</code> today.
      </p>

      <h2>Example composition</h2>
      <p>An overview-to-detail SCADA screen might be described as:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Header: <code>ind-app-header</code> and <code>ind-status-bar</code> for plant time and connection.</li>
        <li>Left: <code>ind-sidebar-nav</code> with <code>ind-tree-node</code> for the area/unit/equipment tree.</li>
        <li>Overview pane: <code>ind-asset-overview</code> and <code>ind-equipment-dashboard</code> summarising each unit's state.</li>
        <li>Detail pane: a faceplate built from <code>ind-pump-card</code>, <code>ind-tank-level-card</code>, <code>ind-value</code> and <code>ind-sparkline</code>.</li>
        <li>Persistent alarm rail: <code>ind-alarm-summary</code> expanding to <code>ind-alarm-panel</code>, with <code>ind-breadcrumb-item</code> showing location.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Components are named for illustration only; nothing is rendered live here.</p>

      <h2>Integration approach</h2>
      <p>
        Use the Web Components directly or through the typed React or Vue wrappers. Live data can be
        mapped to component attributes with the <code>@ind-ds/mqtt</code> helpers where MQTT is
        available, or by your own data layer for other protocols.
      </p>
      <p class="uc-note">
        <strong>Important — what ind-ds is not.</strong> ind-ds does not provide data acquisition, a
        historian, PLC or fieldbus drivers, or a SCADA server. It is the interface layer over a system
        you already run. Tag polling, protocol handling, storage, redundancy, authentication and
        control-network security remain your application's responsibility, and the full
        <code>ind-trend</code> time-series component is planned rather than shipped — use
        <code>ind-sparkline</code> or <code>ind-trend-widget</code> for inline trends today.
      </p>

      <h2>Adoption strategy</h2>
      <p>
        A practical path is to rebuild one process area first: wire its faceplates, values and alarm
        summary, confirm the navigation model with operators, then extend the same patterns to the
        remaining areas. Sharing tokens early keeps new and legacy screens visually coherent during the
        transition.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        Because ind-ds is only the UI layer, it cannot compensate for a missing or unreliable data
        source, and it does not perform alarm rationalisation — the priorities you display are the ones
        your engineering process defines. It is ISA-18.2-aware, not certified. Long-horizon trending
        and historical playback depend on a historian you supply; the current inline trend components
        are intended for recent context, not deep history.
      </p>

      <div class="panel uc-cta">
        <a href={url('/components')} class="btn btn--default">Explore components</a>
        <a href={url('/getting-started')} class="btn btn--ghost">Get started</a>
        <a href={SITE.storybook} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--ghost">Open Storybook →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .uc-detail h2 { margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
  .uc-detail p { max-width: 68ch; }
  .uc-detail ul { max-width: 68ch; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: var(--ind-spacing-2); }
  .uc-detail code { font-family: var(--ind-font-family-mono); font-size: 0.9em; }
  .uc-note { border-left: 3px solid var(--ind-surface-border-strong); padding-left: var(--ind-spacing-4); color: var(--ind-surface-text-secondary); }
  .uc-cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--ind-spacing-3); margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-8); padding: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
</style>
